1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,869
The biggest colossal squid claims usually
describe an animal nobody has measured.

2
00:00:04,869 --> 00:00:10,082
The safest numbers come from specimens,
and the most famous intact specimen

3
00:00:10,082 --> 00:00:13,696
weighed 495 kilograms and measured about
4.2 meters.

4
00:00:13,696 --> 00:00:15,707
How big is a colossal squid?

5
00:00:15,707 --> 00:00:20,821
Colossal squid and giant squid are often
placed in the same headline, but they hold

6
00:00:20,821 --> 00:00:22,485
different kinds of records.

7
00:00:22,485 --> 00:00:27,120
Giant squid can reach greater total length
because their feeding tentacles are

8
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,981
extremely long. Colossal squid are more
compact and much heavier.

9
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:37,157
The best-known preserved female at Te Papa
in New Zealand weighs 495 kilograms and

10
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:39,115
measures about 4.2 meters.

11
00:00:39,115 --> 00:00:44,676
That makes it the heaviest known
invertebrate specimen, not necessarily the

12
00:00:44,676 --> 00:00:46,085
longest cephalopod.

13
00:00:46,085 --> 00:00:50,731
The distinction matters because drawings
often stretch a colossal squid to

14
00:00:50,731 --> 00:00:55,690
giant-squid proportions, while headlines
switch between length and mass without

15
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:56,192
warning.

16
00:00:56,192 --> 00:01:01,876
Specimens also change after capture and
preservation. Tentacles can be missing.

17
00:01:01,876 --> 00:01:03,099
Tissue contracts.

18
00:01:03,099 --> 00:01:07,116
Measurements taken from an intact animal
are more useful than estimates

19
00:01:07,116 --> 00:01:08,587
reconstructed from a beak.

20
00:01:08,587 --> 00:01:13,407
The first open loop is visual: why would
an animal living in deep Antarctic water

21
00:01:13,407 --> 00:01:18,287
invest so much biological material in eyes
that can measure roughly 27 centimeters

22
00:01:18,287 --> 00:01:18,704
across?

23
00:01:18,704 --> 00:01:22,987
The hardest part of this script was
refusing the biggest number on the

24
00:01:22,987 --> 00:01:26,747
A measured specimen and a projected
maximum are not the same thing.

25
00:01:26,747 --> 00:01:32,293
Te Papa's anatomical work describes
colossal squid eyes as the largest animal

26
00:01:32,293 --> 00:01:33,589
eyes ever studied.

27
00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:39,355
In a living animal, the diameter is
estimated around 27 centimeters, with

28
00:01:39,355 --> 00:01:41,883
pupils several centimeters wide.

29
00:01:41,883 --> 00:01:47,157
Large eyes collect more light, but simple
brightness is not the whole explanation.

30
00:01:47,157 --> 00:01:52,387
Modeling work has suggested that very
large deep-sea eyes may be especially

31
00:01:52,387 --> 00:01:57,198
useful for detecting the broad
bioluminescent disturbance made when a

32
00:01:57,198 --> 00:02:00,475
sperm whale moves through surrounding
plankton.

33
00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:03,568
The specimen evidence proves the eye is
enormous.

34
00:02:03,568 --> 00:02:08,430
The exact ecological advantage is still
tested through models and comparative

35
00:02:08,430 --> 00:02:13,355
anatomy because healthy colossal squid are
not routinely observed in the wild.

36
00:02:13,355 --> 00:02:17,900
The eyes also carry light organs near
them, an unusual arrangement whose

37
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:19,984
behavior is not fully documented.

38
00:02:19,984 --> 00:02:25,051
This is a useful example of evidence
layers: direct measurement of the organ,

39
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:30,183
strong optical principles, and a more
tentative hypothesis about the signal it

40
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:30,709
detects.

41
00:02:30,709 --> 00:02:34,768
The next question is what the squid does
when the target is close enough to strike.

42
00:02:34,768 --> 00:02:38,741
The colossal squid's tentacle clubs carry
swiveling hooks.

43
00:02:38,741 --> 00:02:42,645
Some can rotate in their sockets, while
others are fixed.

44
00:02:42,645 --> 00:02:46,032
The arms carry suckers and smaller hooks.

45
00:02:46,032 --> 00:02:51,400
The beak is robust, as expected for a
large predatory cephalopod.

46
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,658
These structures are available for direct
study because specimens have been

47
00:02:55,658 --> 00:02:57,645
recovered from Antarctic fisheries.

48
00:02:57,645 --> 00:03:01,800
What is missing is a complete hunting
sequence from a healthy adult at depth.

49
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:06,729
It is tempting to animate the hooks like
grappling equipment and assign a dramatic

50
00:03:06,729 --> 00:03:10,936
attack speed, but the anatomy does not
provide a full behavior script.

51
00:03:10,936 --> 00:03:16,104
Stomach contents, predator remains, and
distribution records suggest prey such as

52
00:03:16,104 --> 00:03:19,422
Antarctic fishes and interactions with
sperm whales.

53
00:03:19,422 --> 00:03:23,251
Scars on whales show that large squids are
not passive prey.

54
00:03:23,251 --> 00:03:28,981
Still, the balance between ambush, active
pursuit, and scavenging is not resolved by

55
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:30,755
one body in a museum tank.

56
00:03:30,755 --> 00:03:35,470
The open loop becomes a scientific one:
how do you describe an animal accurately

57
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:40,008
when nearly every close look begins after
it has left its normal environment?

58
00:03:40,008 --> 00:03:45,430
Te Papa's preserved colossal squid has
supported repeated examinations of eyes,

59
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:48,312
hooks, beak, tissue, and internal anatomy.

60
00:03:48,312 --> 00:03:51,490
Samples can be revisited as methods
improve.

61
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:55,101
That makes the specimen a durable evidence
object.

62
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:59,513
It does not show migration routes,
courtship, hunting posture, or lifespan.

63
00:03:59,513 --> 00:04:04,278
Fisheries records tell researchers where
encounters occur, but encounter location

64
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:06,808
is not always the animal's preferred
depth.

65
00:04:06,808 --> 00:04:11,108
Beaks in sperm-whale stomachs reveal
predator-prey relationships, but soft

66
00:04:11,108 --> 00:04:15,176
tissue has already vanished. Each method
answers a different question.

67
00:04:15,176 --> 00:04:19,214
The right conclusion is not that the
colossal squid is unknowable.

68
00:04:19,214 --> 00:04:23,741
We know its mass can exceed any other
confirmed invertebrate, its eyes are

69
00:04:23,741 --> 00:04:27,045
extraordinary, and its tentacles carry
rotating hooks.

70
00:04:27,045 --> 00:04:31,264
We also know the viral life story is much
more complete than the evidence.

71
00:04:31,264 --> 00:04:33,336
A good episode preserves that gap.

72
00:04:33,336 --> 00:04:38,149
The next subject returns to the deepest
trench, where the historical record has

73
00:04:38,149 --> 00:04:42,902
the opposite problem: many modern dives
have made an old four-person statistic

74
00:04:42,902 --> 00:04:43,451
obsolete.

75
00:04:43,451 --> 00:04:47,331
Colossal squid and giant squid are
different animals.

76
00:04:47,331 --> 00:04:52,822
Colossal squid have a heavier body,
rotating hooks on parts of the arms and

77
00:04:52,822 --> 00:04:56,043
tentacles, a beak, and extremely large
eyes.

78
00:04:56,043 --> 00:04:59,264
But live behavior remains poorly
documented.

79
00:04:59,264 --> 00:05:05,168
Museums can measure a specimen's mass,
mantle, arms, tentacles, beak, and eyes.

80
00:05:05,168 --> 00:05:10,382
Estimates of a theoretical maximum use
incomplete specimens, beaks found in

81
00:05:10,382 --> 00:05:14,413
predator stomachs, and assumptions about
body proportions.

82
00:05:14,413 --> 00:05:19,091
Those projections may be useful, but they
should not be narrated as if a complete

83
00:05:19,091 --> 00:05:20,765
animal was placed on a scale.

84
00:05:20,765 --> 00:05:25,526
Te Papa's displayed specimen is useful
precisely because it can be measured,

85
00:05:25,526 --> 00:05:28,408
re-examined, and compared with other
material.

86
00:05:28,408 --> 00:05:33,078
Its reported mass and total length are
real specimen values, but even they

87
00:05:33,078 --> 00:05:34,088
require context.

88
00:05:34,088 --> 00:05:38,351
The animal was frozen, thawed, unfolded,
and preserved.

89
00:05:38,351 --> 00:05:41,219
Tentacles can contract or be damaged.

90
00:05:41,219 --> 00:05:43,219
Water and ice can affect mass.

91
00:05:43,219 --> 00:05:48,552
Total length, mantle length, and mass
therefore answer different size questions.

92
00:05:48,552 --> 00:05:51,079
Projected maxima are a separate category.

93
00:05:51,079 --> 00:05:55,825
Larger beaks recovered from sperm whale
stomachs suggest that larger colossal

94
00:05:55,825 --> 00:06:00,878
squid exist, but the relationship between
beak size and total body mass is not yet

95
00:06:00,878 --> 00:06:03,960
supported by a large sample of complete
specimens.

96
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:08,557
The video should never place a projected
giant beside the measured specimen without

97
00:06:08,557 --> 00:06:09,000
a label.

98
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,304
'Measured,' 'estimated,' and 'possible'
must look different on screen.

99
00:06:13,304 --> 00:06:18,468
That visual honesty lets the animal remain
extraordinary without borrowing certainty

100
00:06:18,468 --> 00:06:20,067
from an incomplete record.

101
00:06:20,067 --> 00:06:24,699
The last measurement card should show
mass, total length, and mantle length as

102
00:06:24,699 --> 00:06:29,213
separate fields, with a note that
preservation and damage affect comparison.

103
00:06:29,213 --> 00:06:31,999
A projected maximum belongs on a different
card.

104
00:06:31,999 --> 00:06:36,699
The audience can still imagine a larger
animal; the video simply refuses to label

105
00:06:36,699 --> 00:06:38,904
that imagination as a specimen record.

106
00:06:38,904 --> 00:06:43,304
Use 'measured specimen' whenever the
495-kilogram value appears.

107
00:06:43,304 --> 00:06:48,632
Build the episode around a measurement
table rather than a sea-monster

108
00:06:48,632 --> 00:06:53,923
Mark which values are directly measured,
which come from a specimen, and which are

109
00:06:53,923 --> 00:06:54,504
inferred.

110
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:56,100
Use original diagrams.

111
00:06:56,100 --> 00:07:01,615
Te Papa's pages are excellent factual
references, but at least one displayed

112
00:07:01,615 --> 00:07:07,202
specimen image is marked CC BY-NC-ND,
which is not acceptable for a monetized

113
00:07:07,202 --> 00:07:08,291
modified video.

114
00:07:08,291 --> 00:07:13,337
Size records depend on whether the claim
refers to mass, total length, mantle

115
00:07:13,337 --> 00:07:14,909
length, or eye diameter.

116
00:07:14,909 --> 00:07:18,885
Damaged and contracted specimens
complicate length.

117
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:25,433
The 495-kilogram, roughly 4.2-meter animal
is a measured specimen associated with Te

118
00:07:25,433 --> 00:07:28,707
Papa; it does not prove a species maximum.

119
00:07:28,707 --> 00:07:33,122
Claims that colossal squid have the
largest eyes should remain tied to

120
00:07:33,122 --> 00:07:38,294
measured or documented specimens and not
become a universal exact record without a

121
00:07:38,294 --> 00:07:39,240
current source.

122
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:43,330
The colossal squid is enormous without
borrowing a fictional maximum.

123
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:47,835
The most honest version separates the
animal on the table from the animal we

124
00:07:47,835 --> 00:07:52,280
imagine in the dark—and tells the viewer
which number belongs to which one.
